@startuml agriasic_digital_v2_measurement_sequence
title AgriASIC Digital - Measurement Transaction Sequence (Rev 5, tetrapolar, Ibex)

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center
skinparam responseMessageBelowArrow true
skinparam ParticipantPadding 14
skinparam BoxPadding 8
skinparam defaultFontSize 14

actor FW as "Firmware on Ibex"
participant MMIO as "agriasic_rv32i_mmio"
participant SHELL as "agriasic_rv32i_control_shell"
participant FSM as "measurement_fsm"
participant EXC as "excitation_ctrl"
participant SAR as "sar_controller"
participant AFE as "Analog front end\n(sine DAC, fixed-gain PGA, prog. TIA, S/H, SAR ADC)"

== Configuration and launch ==
FW -> MMIO : write PAIR_LOG2, SETTLE, DIVIDER, CONV
FW -> MMIO : write AFE_CTRL (tia_rf, amplitude, mux_settle)
MMIO -> SHELL : cfg_* updates
MMIO -> AFE : afe_tia_rf_o[2:0]\n(2-bit code decoded to 3 switch wires)\nThe diff PGA gain is FIXED - no digital control

FW -> MMIO : write CTRL.START
MMIO -> SHELL : start_pulse_o (one cycle, differentiated from the write)
SHELL -> FSM : start
note right of MMIO
A START arriving while busy is REJECTED, not queued: the FSM
only samples start in S_IDLE, so such a write used to vanish
with no way for firmware to tell. It now sets the sticky
STATUS[7] start_rejected flag. To restart early, write
CTRL.ABORT first -- see the abort path at the end.
end note
FW -> FW : execute wfi -- core clock gates off
note right of FW
The core sleeps for the whole measurement.
Measured: idle 99.98% of a three-point sweep.
end note

FSM -> EXC : exc_enable_o = 1
EXC -> AFE : afe_sine_code_o[7:0]\n16-point COSINE, one code per phase state
EXC --> FSM : phase_index_i, period_tick_i

== Settling window ==
FSM -> FSM : count settle_cycles_i EXCITATION PERIODS\n(not raw cycles)

== Accumulation loop: 4 phase points x 2 channels = 8 conversions per pair ==
loop pair_count_q < 2^pair_log2_i
  loop point = PT_0, PT_180, PT_90, PT_270
    FSM -> EXC : watch for the target phase index
    EXC --> FSM : phase_index_i matches

    FSM -> SAR : sample_req_o, take_sample_o = 1, channel_o = CH_DV
    SAR -> AFE : afe_sample_o -- ONE strobe, freezes BOTH S/H
    note right of AFE
      The voltage/current phase relationship is set by
      THIS INSTANT. Skew between the two conversions
      below therefore costs nothing.
    end note

    SAR -> AFE : afe_mux_sel_o = 0 (dV), wait mux_settle
    loop 8 bit trials, MSB first
      SAR -> AFE : afe_adc_dac_o trial code
      AFE --> SAR : afe_adc_comp_i (timed, unsynchronised)
    end
    SAR --> FSM : sample_done_o, code_o = dV, overrange_o

    FSM -> SAR : sample_req_o, take_sample_o = 0, channel_o = CH_CUR
    SAR -> AFE : afe_mux_sel_o = 1 (I), no new strobe
    loop 8 bit trials, MSB first
      SAR -> AFE : afe_adc_dac_o trial code
      AFE --> SAR : afe_adc_comp_i
    end
    SAR --> FSM : sample_done_o, code_o = I, overrange_o

    FSM -> FSM : accumulate into dv_i/dv_q/cur_i/cur_q\nby point sign, latch per-channel overrange
  end
  FSM -> FSM : pair_count_q++
end

== Completion and result snapshot ==
FSM -> FSM : snapshot all FOUR accumulators into shadows\non the single cycle busy_o drops
note right of FSM
A read landing mid-run returns the previous COMPLETE
result, never a partial sum -- and dV and I always come
from the same run, which matters because they are
divided against each other.
end note
FSM --> SHELL : done_o (one cycle)
SHELL --> MMIO : sticky DONE latch set
MMIO --> FW : irq_fast[0] -- core leaves wfi
note right of MMIO
mie.fast0 is set but mstatus.MIE is not, so the core
RESUMES at the next instruction rather than taking a
trap. No handler, no context save.
end note

== Firmware reads results ==
FW -> MMIO : read STATUS (busy, done, overrange dV / I)
FW -> MMIO : read RESULT_I, RESULT_Q, RESULT_CUR_I, RESULT_CUR_Q
MMIO --> FW : four signed 16-bit accumulators
FW -> FW : store per point, with AFE_CTRL and overrange flags
note right of FW
Z(f) = dV(f) / I(f) is computed HOST-side, never on die.
The gain settings must be stored alongside the results:
the two channels have independent gains, so they do not
cancel in the ratio.
end note

== Abort: the only way to cut a run short (host / debugger side) ==
note over FW, AFE
Reachable from ANY state above, not only from here.
end note
FW -> MMIO : write CTRL.ABORT
MMIO -> SHELL : abort_pulse_o (one cycle)
SHELL -> FSM : abort_i
SHELL -> SAR : abort_i
FSM -> FSM : state -> S_IDLE (overrides every state)
SAR -> SAR : state -> S_IDLE, drop busy / adc_enable / sample_done
MMIO -> MMIO : clear busy. NO done pulse, NO irq_fast[0]
note right of MMIO
Abort emits no done, so an aborted run can never be read back as
a completed one -- and so it cannot wake a core already parked in
wfi. That is why abort is a host- and debugger-side control (the
host SPI port, or the debug module's system bus), not part of the
firmware's own wait path.

The SAR is aborted as well as the FSM on purpose: a conversion
left in flight would otherwise run to completion and pulse
sample_done after the FSM had gone idle, where the NEXT run would
consume it as its own first sample.

The accumulators are cleared by the next START rather than by the
abort, so the last complete result stays readable.

ABORT wins over START when one write asserts both, and the dropped
start is still flagged in STATUS[7]. Without that precedence the
two disagreed: the bridge cleared busy while the FSM, which only
honours abort outside S_IDLE, accepted the start -- the engine
would run with STATUS reporting idle.
end note

@enduml
